#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
fake_static_ransom_lin.py - static Linux fake ransomware

A fake ransomware lock screen.  It is a prank: nothing is encrypted,
nothing is deleted, and the only way out is the passphrase below (or
killing the process / switching to a TTY).

This is the still-wallpaper build: the frame is frozen.  Nothing moves,
flickers or blinks, and the window only repaints when you type or when
the ACCESS DENIED banner appears/disappears.

On a 16:9 screen the whole monitor is filled with the exact 1920x1080
still produced by locked_skull_v3.py, with its bottom "Locked by
BossDefender" caption.  On any other shape (16:10, ultrawide, portrait,
or an odd resolution) the skull and the caption are recombined so the
frame still fills the entire window edge to edge: the skull keeps its
shape and is scaled to the space above the caption, and the caption is
scaled with the screen.  Nothing is stretched and no letterbox bars
appear.  A brief "Summoning the skull..." screen appears first, just like
the source.  The idle artwork has no visible password field: typed
characters appear as small dots at the very bottom, and a wrong passphrase
briefly replaces the caption area with ACCESS DENIED.  It is drawn with
Cairo, so it needs PyGObject and GTK 3 or GTK 4, exactly like
fake_ransom.py.  GTK 3 is preferred on X11 because only GTK 3 can hold a
keyboard grab; otherwise GTK 4 is used.  Set FAKE_RANSOM_GTK=3 or 4 to
force a version.

Every active monitor gets its own fullscreen prank window, scaled to that
monitor's own size and shape, so a laptop with an external display or a
multi-monitor desktop is covered from edge to edge no matter how different
the screens are.  Only the primary monitor's window accepts keyboard
input; the other windows are display-only and mirror the typed password
and the ACCESS DENIED banner.

While running, only letters, digits, Backspace, Shift and Enter are
accepted.  Everything else is swallowed: Meta/Windows, Ctrl, Alt, Tab,
Esc, Print Screen, Delete, Insert, Home, End, arrows, Page Up/Down,
the function keys, Space, Caps Lock and punctuation.

On X11 the keyboard grab also stops the window manager from acting on
Super and Alt+Tab.  On Wayland, GTK 4.16 or newer asks the compositor
to inhibit its system shortcuts while the prank is focused, so Super /
Meta, Alt+Tab and similar combinations are delivered to the password
filter instead of opening the desktop's launcher or switcher.

On Hyprland, the fullscreen prank additionally enters a temporary
keybind submap with no useful bindings.  This stops Omarchy's global
menu, workspace, close-all and lock shortcuts even on builds where a
compositor bind is not covered by the protocol inhibitor.  The previous
submap is restored on exit, and a detached watchdog restores it if the
prank process is killed.

Run:
    python3 fake_static_ransom_lin.py

Test without opening a window:
    python3 fake_static_ransom_lin.py --selftest
    python3 fake_static_ransom_lin.py --render-test /tmp/fake_ransom.png

Windowed test (does not cover the desktop):
    python3 fake_static_ransom_lin.py --windowed 1280x720 --duration 5

Password: bomboclat
"""

